# Karpathy coding guidelines (this repo)

Any agent that **implements or edits production code** in this project must follow these
principles, distilled from [Andrej Karpathy's notes on LLM coding](https://x.com/karpathy/status/2015883857489522876)
and the [karpathy-guidelines](https://github.com/multica-ai/andrej-karpathy-skills/blob/main/skills/karpathy-guidelines/SKILL.md)
skill. Bias toward caution over speed; use judgment on trivial one-liners.

## Think before coding

- State assumptions explicitly; if uncertain, ask instead of guessing.
- When multiple interpretations exist, surface them and tradeoffs—do not pick silently.
- If something is unclear or inconsistent with the codebase, stop and name it before coding.

## Simplicity first

- Ship the **minimum** code that satisfies the request—no speculative features, flags, or
  “future-proof” abstractions.
- No helpers or layers used only once; no error handling for impossible cases.
- If the diff is much larger than the problem warrants, simplify before finishing.

## Surgical changes

- Touch only what the task requires; do not “improve” adjacent code, comments, or formatting.
- Match existing conventions in the file and project.
- Remove orphans **your** change created (unused imports, dead branches); do not delete
  pre-existing dead code unless asked.
- Every changed line should trace to the user's request.

## Goal-driven execution

- Turn work into **verifiable** success criteria (tests, lint, build, or explicit checks).
- For bugs and features, prefer reproduce → fix → verify over “should work now.”
- Run the relevant project commands (`pnpm test`, `pnpm lint`, etc.) before claiming done.
